Streams shouldn't set `content-length` at all, should they?

```js
const stream = require('stream');
const express = require('express')();
const fastify = require('fastify')();

express.get('/hello', (_, res) => {
  stream.Readable.from('hi there!').pipe(res);
});

fastify.get('/hello', () => {
  return stream.Readable.from('hi there!');
});

express.listen(3000);
fastify.listen(3001);
```

```sh-session                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    
$ curl -i http://localhost:3000/hello
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
X-Powered-By: Express
Date: Mon, 02 Nov 2020 09:04:02 GMT
Connection: keep-alive
Keep-Alive: timeout=5
Transfer-Encoding: chunked

hi there!%
$ curl -I http://localhost:3000/hello
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
X-Powered-By: Express
Date: Mon, 02 Nov 2020 09:05:22 GMT
Connection: keep-alive
Keep-Alive: timeout=5

$ curl -i http://localhost:3001/hello
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
content-type: application/octet-stream
Date: Mon, 02 Nov 2020 09:04:00 GMT
Connection: keep-alive
Keep-Alive: timeout=5
Transfer-Encoding: chunked

hi there!%
```

Seems `express` does not set `content-type` at all. But the difference in `express` between `GET` and `HEAD` seems to be that `HEAD` does _not_ include `Transfer-Encoding`. Either way, no `content-length`

Thanks @MetCoder95!

I've found a bug with it. The bug itself is pretty straightforward, but I wonder if there's also a bug in Fastify here?

The bug is `reply.code(200).send();` - i.e. no body at all. Changing this line

https://github.com/MetCoder95/fastify-get-head/blob/a0818b9d2772bb561273fe625b9df2989464672b/index.js#L23

to `function onSendHandler (req, reply, payload = '', done) {` "fixes" that issue (dunno if it's the correct fix, checking `payload` for truthiness and setting `0` as content length if falsy is probably better).

BUT, this error is silently swallowed by Fastify.

```js
const fastify = require('fastify')();

fastify.route({
  path: '/hello',
  handler(_, reply) {
    reply.code(200).send();
  },
  method: 'GET',
  onSend() {
    throw new Error('boom');
  },
});

fastify.listen(3000);
```

Calling this makes the request hang forever. Another "fix"

```js
onSend(_, __, ___, done) {
  done(new Error('boom'));
},
```

but I think if a hook throws Fastify should automatically handle that?
